      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Hyperthyroidism associated with subacute (painful, viral) thyroiditis is well-recognized as a clinical entity; the thyroid gland in Graves disease is minimally, if ever, tender and painful. We describe a 10-year-old girl with hyperthyroidism whose initial clinical presentation was predominantly a painful, tender goiter. Graves disease was established by high uptake of 131I with a diffuse pattern of distribution of radioactivity on scan and the presence of thyroid-stimulating antibody. Thyrotropin-binding inhibiting IgG and antibody to thyroid microsomal antigen were both positive. She responded well to treatment with propylthiouracil and had spontaneous regression of her thyroid pain. The cause of the severe pain and tenderness remains speculative."},
